                    "# OPF Scale-Recursive Measurement Framework Notebook\n\n\
This notebook formalizes the user's OPF-inspired hypotheses as a **symbolic \
and testable mathematical framework** in the Wolfram Language.\n\n## \
Important rigor note\n\nThe constructions below treat claims about \
sub-Planckian continuation, timeless self-measurement, and consciousness as \
**hypotheses to be formalized and tested for internal consistency**. They are \
**not asserted as established physics**.\n\n## Goals\n\n1. Define a \
scale-extended OPF action.\n2. Derive Euler-Lagrange equations with respect \
to ``rho[x, sigma]``, ``chi[x, sigma]``, and ``res[x, sigma]``.\n3. Construct \
fixed-point and scale-invariant conditions.\n4. Linearize around stationary \
backgrounds and compute modal stability conditions.\n5. Define a \
self-measurement kernel and fixed-point equation.\n6. Scan ratio candidates \
including the golden ratio and golden angle.\n7. Identify adjacent \
mathematical and physical domains this framework could influence.\n\n---\n\n\
## 1. Global assumptions and symbols\n\n```wl\nClearAll[\"Global`*\"];\n\n\
$Assumptions = And @@ {\n   alpha > 0, beta > 0, gamma > 0,\n   eta > 0, zeta \
> 0, xi > 0,\n   delta \[Element] Reals, lambda \[Element] Reals,\n   x \
\[Element] Reals, sigma \[Element] Reals,\n   phi > 1\n};\n\n\
Needs[\"VariationalMethods`\"];\n```\n\nWe use:\n\n- ``x`` for spatial \
coordinate,\n- ``sigma`` for logarithmic scale,\n- ``rho[x, sigma]`` for \
information density,\n- ``chi[x, sigma]`` for field complexity,\n- ``res[x, \
sigma]`` for observational resolution.\n\nThe logarithmic scale coordinate is \
intended to encode recursive similarity under multiplicative changes of \
physical scale.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Define the scale-extended Layer 1 action\n\n\
We start from the OPF Layer 1 density\n\n$$\\rho \\log(\\chi) + \\chi R^2$$\n\
\nand minimally extend it by adding spatial and scale regularization together \
with cubic coupling:\n\n$$\\mathcal{L} = \\rho\\log\\chi + \\chi\\,res^2 + \
\\alpha (\\partial_\\sigma \\rho)^2 + \\beta (\\partial_\\sigma \\chi)^2 + \
\\gamma (\\partial_\\sigma res)^2 + \\eta (\\partial_x \\rho)^2 + \\zeta \
(\\partial_x \\chi)^2 + \\xi (\\partial_x res)^2 + \
\\delta\\,\\rho\\,\\chi\\,res + \\lambda\\,\\rho^2$$\n\nThe final ``lambda \
rho^2`` term is included as a simple confining term to help define stationary \
backgrounds.\n\n```wl\nlagrangianDensity = \n  rho[x, sigma] Log[chi[x, \
sigma]] + chi[x, sigma] res[x, sigma]^2 +\n   alpha D[rho[x, sigma], sigma]^2 \
+ beta D[chi[x, sigma], sigma]^2 +\n   gamma D[res[x, sigma], sigma]^2 + eta \
D[rho[x, sigma], x]^2 +\n   zeta D[chi[x, sigma], x]^2 + xi D[res[x, sigma], \
x]^2 +\n   delta rho[x, sigma] chi[x, sigma] res[x, sigma] +\n   lambda \
rho[x, sigma]^2;\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Euler-Lagrange equations\n\nWe derive \
the field equations symbolically using \
[VariationalD](paclet:ref/VariationalD).\n\n```wl\neulerRho = FullSimplify[\n \
  VariationalD[lagrangianDensity, rho[x, sigma], {x, sigma}][[1]],\n   \
Assumptions -> $Assumptions\n   ];\n\neulerChi = FullSimplify[\n   \
VariationalD[lagrangianDensity, chi[x, sigma], {x, sigma}][[1]],\n   \
Assumptions -> $Assumptions\n   ];\n\neulerRes = FullSimplify[\n   \
VariationalD[lagrangianDensity, res[x, sigma], {x, sigma}][[1]],\n   \
Assumptions -> $Assumptions\n   ];\n\nfieldEquations = Thread[{eulerRho, \
eulerChi, eulerRes} == 0];\nfieldEquations // TableForm\n```\n\nThese are the \
scale-spatial OPF equations of motion.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Stationary homogeneous \
sector\n\nTo study exact fixed points, we first restrict to scale- and \
space-independent backgrounds:\n\n```wl\nhomogeneousRules = {\n   rho[x, \
sigma] -> rho0,\n   chi[x, sigma] -> chi0,\n   res[x, sigma] -> res0,\n   \
Derivative[1, 0][rho][x, sigma] -> 0,\n   Derivative[0, 1][rho][x, sigma] -> \
0,\n   Derivative[1, 0][chi][x, sigma] -> 0,\n   Derivative[0, 1][chi][x, \
sigma] -> 0,\n   Derivative[1, 0][res][x, sigma] -> 0,\n   Derivative[0, \
1][res][x, sigma] -> 0,\n   Derivative[2, 0][rho][x, sigma] -> 0,\n   \
Derivative[0, 2][rho][x, sigma] -> 0,\n   Derivative[2, 0][chi][x, sigma] -> \
0,\n   Derivative[0, 2][chi][x, sigma] -> 0,\n   Derivative[2, 0][res][x, \
sigma] -> 0,\n   Derivative[0, 2][res][x, sigma] -> 0\n};\n\n\
homogeneousEquations = FullSimplify[fieldEquations /. homogeneousRules];\n\
homogeneousEquations // TableForm\n```\n\nSolve for constant backgrounds:\n\n\
```wl\nfixedPoints = FullSimplify[\n   Reduce[homogeneousEquations, {rho0, \
chi0, res0}, Reals, \n    MaxExtraConditions -> All],\n   Assumptions -> chi0 \
> 0\n   ];\nfixedPoints\n```\n\nThis gives the algebraic fixed-point \
structure that stands in for the timeless self-consistent field background.\n\
\n---\n\n## 5. Self-similar scale ansatz\n\nTo model recursive repetition \
through all scales, introduce a multiplicative ansatz under logarithmic scale \
translation:\n\n$$f(x, \\sigma + \\Delta) = \\mu_f f(x, \\sigma)$$\n\nA \
simple separable realization is:\n\n\
$$\\rho(x,\\sigma)=\\rho_s(x)e^{a\\sigma},\\quad \
\\chi(x,\\sigma)=\\chi_s(x)e^{b\\sigma},\\quad \
res(x,\\sigma)=res_s(x)e^{c\\sigma}$$\n\n```wl\nscaleAnsatz = {\n   rho[x, \
sigma] -> rhoS[x] Exp[a sigma],\n   chi[x, sigma] -> chiS[x] Exp[b sigma],\n  \
 res[x, sigma] -> resS[x] Exp[c sigma]\n};\n\nscaleReducedEquations = \
FullSimplify[\n   fieldEquations /. scaleAnsatz,\n   Assumptions -> \
Join[$Assumptions, {chiS[x] > 0}]\n   ];\n\nscaleReducedEquations // \
TableForm\n```\n\nA strict scale-invariant sector often requires exponent \
matching among terms. We can extract the exponential powers term-by-term.\n\n\
```wl\nexpPowers[expr_] := Cases[\n   expr,\n   E^(k_. sigma) :> k,\n   \
Infinity\n   ] // DeleteDuplicates;\n\nscalePowers = expPowers /@ (Subtract \
@@@ scaleReducedEquations);\nscalePowers // TableForm\n```\n\nThis helps \
identify consistency conditions such as relations among ``a``, ``b``, and \
``c``.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Linearization around a homogeneous background\n\nWe \
perturb around a constant solution:\n\n$$\\rho = \\rho_0 + \\epsilon u,\\quad \
\\chi = \\chi_0 + \\epsilon v,\\quad res = res_0 + \\epsilon w$$\n\nand \
retain first order in ``epsilon``.\n\n```wl\nlinRules = {\n   rho[x, sigma] \
-> rho0 + epsilon u[x, sigma],\n   chi[x, sigma] -> chi0 + epsilon v[x, \
sigma],\n   res[x, sigma] -> res0 + epsilon w[x, sigma]\n};\n\n\
linearizedEquations = Normal @ Series[\n     fieldEquations /. linRules,\n    \
 {epsilon, 0, 1}\n     ] /. Equal -> Subtract;\n\nlinearizedFirstOrder = \
FullSimplify[\n   Coefficient[#, epsilon, 1] == 0 & /@ linearizedEquations,\n \
  Assumptions -> Join[$Assumptions, {chi0 > 0}]\n   ];\n\n\
linearizedFirstOrder // TableForm\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Plane-wave modal \
analysis and stability\n\nWe now use a joint spatial-scale harmonic ansatz\n\n\
$$u = u_0 e^{i(kx+q\\sigma)},\\quad v = v_0 e^{i(kx+q\\sigma)},\\quad w = w_0 \
e^{i(kx+q\\sigma)}$$\n\nwhich converts the linearized PDE system into an \
algebraic matrix equation.\n\n```wl\nmodalRules = {\n   u[x, sigma] -> u0 \
Exp[I (k x + q sigma)],\n   v[x, sigma] -> v0 Exp[I (k x + q sigma)],\n   \
w[x, sigma] -> w0 Exp[I (k x + q sigma)]\n};\n\nmodalSystem = FullSimplify[\n \
  linearizedFirstOrder /. modalRules,\n   Assumptions -> Join[$Assumptions, \
{k \[Element] Reals, q \[Element] Reals, chi0 > 0}]\n   ];\n\n\
modalPolynomialSystem = modalSystem /. {\n    Derivative[1, 0][u][x, sigma] \
:> I k u0 Exp[I (k x + q sigma)],\n    Derivative[0, 1][u][x, sigma] :> I q \
u0 Exp[I (k x + q sigma)],\n    Derivative[2, 0][u][x, sigma] :> -k^2 u0 \
Exp[I (k x + q sigma)],\n    Derivative[0, 2][u][x, sigma] :> -q^2 u0 Exp[I \
(k x + q sigma)],\n    Derivative[1, 0][v][x, sigma] :> I k v0 Exp[I (k x + q \
sigma)],\n    Derivative[0, 1][v][x, sigma] :> I q v0 Exp[I (k x + q sigma)],\
\n    Derivative[2, 0][v][x, sigma] :> -k^2 v0 Exp[I (k x + q sigma)],\n    \
Derivative[0, 2][v][x, sigma] :> -q^2 v0 Exp[I (k x + q sigma)],\n    \
Derivative[1, 0][w][x, sigma] :> I k w0 Exp[I (k x + q sigma)],\n    \
Derivative[0, 1][w][x, sigma] :> I q w0 Exp[I (k x + q sigma)],\n    \
Derivative[2, 0][w][x, sigma] :> -k^2 w0 Exp[I (k x + q sigma)],\n    \
Derivative[0, 2][w][x, sigma] :> -q^2 w0 Exp[I (k x + q sigma)]\n    };\n\n\
modalAlgebraic = FullSimplify[\n   DivideSides[#, Exp[I (k x + q sigma)]] & \
/@ modalPolynomialSystem,\n   Assumptions -> Join[$Assumptions, {k \[Element] \
Reals, q \[Element] Reals, chi0 > 0}]\n   ];\n\nmodalMatrix = \
CoefficientArrays[\n    Subtract @@@ modalAlgebraic,\n    {u0, v0, w0}\n    \
][[2]] // Normal // FullSimplify;\n\nmodalMatrix // MatrixForm\n```\n\nThe \
stability condition is the vanishing determinant:\n\n```wl\n\
dispersionRelation = FullSimplify[\n   Det[modalMatrix],\n   Assumptions -> \
Join[$Assumptions, {k \[Element] Reals, q \[Element] Reals, chi0 > 0}]\n   ];\
\n\ndispersionRelation\n```\n\nThis gives the OPF dispersion polynomial \
governing stable or unstable recursive modes.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Undamped \
harmonic sector\n\nFollowing the user\[CloseCurlyQuote]s directive, stable \
resonant modes are those with non-decaying oscillatory behavior. In this \
static scale-harmonic setting that means solving the dispersion relation for \
real ``q`` and ``k`` under background consistency.\n\n```wl\n\
undampedModeConditions = Reduce[\n   dispersionRelation == 0,\n   q,\n   \
Reals,\n   MaxExtraConditions -> All\n   ];\n\nundampedModeConditions\n```\n\n\
If one later introduces an emergent time variable ``t``, then the same \
machinery can be repeated with time-harmonic modes and actual damping rates.\n\
\n---\n\n## 9. Measurement operator and self-consistency\n\nTo encode \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]measurement measures itself,\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
we define a self-measurement operator on a generic field ``psi``:\n\n\
$$\\mathcal{Q}[\\psi](x,\\sigma)=\\int\\int \
K(x,\\sigma,xp,sigmap)\\,\\psi(xp,sigmap)\\,dxp\\,d\\sigma_p$$\n\nThe \
fixed-point equation is\n\n$$\\psi = \\mathcal{Q}[\\psi]$$\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[psi, kernel, xp, sigmap];\nmeasurementOperator[psi_] := \n  \
Integrate[\n   kernel[x, sigma, xp, sigmap] psi[xp, sigmap],\n   {xp, \
-Infinity, Infinity}, {sigmap, -Infinity, Infinity},\n   Assumptions -> \
$Assumptions\n   ];\n\nselfMeasurementEquation = psi[x, sigma] == \
measurementOperator[psi];\nselfMeasurementEquation\n```\n\nA Gaussian example \
kernel that is local in space-scale but nontrivial in cross-scale reach:\n\n\
```wl\nClearAll[sx, ss, omega, theta];\nexampleKernel = \n  Exp[-(x - \
xp)^2/(2 sx^2)] Exp[-(sigma - sigmap)^2/(2 ss^2)] \n    Cos[omega (sigma - \
sigmap) + theta (x - xp)];\n```\n\nThis kernel can be substituted into \
``measurementOperator`` and studied for fixed points.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Ratio \
map: golden ratio, golden angle, and candidate invariants\n\nWe define \
canonical constants:\n\n```wl\ngoldenRatio = FullSimplify[(1 + Sqrt[5])/2];\n\
goldenAngle = FullSimplify[2 Pi (1 - 1/goldenRatio)];\n{goldenRatio, \
goldenAngle}\n```\n\nWe also define a ratio-quality functional based on \
irrationality via short continued-fraction truncations. This is not a proof \
of physical relevance, but a principled way to rank candidate scale ratios \
for non-resonant recursive packing.\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[continuedFractionScore];\ncontinuedFractionScore[r_?NumericQ, \
n_Integer : 12] := Module[{cf},\n  cf = ContinuedFraction[r, n];\n  \
Total[1/(1 + Rest[cf]^2)]\n  ];\n```\n\nGenerate a set of candidate ratios:\n\
\n```wl\ncandidateRatios = <|\n   \"GoldenRatio\" -> N[goldenRatio, 50],\n   \
\"SilverRatio\" -> N[1 + Sqrt[2], 50],\n   \"PlasticConstant\" -> N[Root[x^3 \
- x - 1 &, 1], 50],\n   \"EulerE\" -> N[E, 50],\n   \"Pi\" -> N[Pi, 50],\n   \
\"GoldenAngleOver2Pi\" -> N[goldenAngle/(2 Pi), 50]\n   |>;\n\nratioScores = \
AssociationMap[continuedFractionScore, candidateRatios];\nratioScores\n```\n\n\
A more OPF-specific test is to substitute scale-step values into a discrete \
self-similarity condition.\n\nFor a field scaling rule\n\n\
$$f(\\sigma+\\Delta)=\\mu f(\\sigma)$$\n\none may scan ``Delta = Log[r]`` for \
candidate ratios ``r``.\n\n```wl\nratioSteps = AssociationMap[Log, \
candidateRatios];\nratioSteps\n```\n\n---\n\n## 11. Discrete scale recursion \
operator\n\nA minimal recursive map for the homogeneous sector can be \
introduced as:\n\n$$\\begin{pmatrix}\\rho_{n+1}\\\\ \\chi_{n+1}\\\\ res_{n+1}\
\\end{pmatrix} = \n\\mathcal{F}\\left(\\begin{pmatrix}\\rho_n\\\\ \\chi_n\\\\ \
res_n\\end{pmatrix}; r\\right)$$\n\nwith a ratio parameter ``r`` controlling \
interscale step size.\n\n```wl\nClearAll[opfMap];\nopfMap[{rhoN_, chiN_, \
resN_}, r_] := {\n   rhoN + r (Log[chiN] + delta chiN resN + 2 lambda rhoN),\n\
   chiN + r (rhoN/chiN + resN^2 + delta rhoN resN),\n   resN + r (2 chiN resN \
+ delta rhoN chiN)\n   };\n```\n\nFixed points satisfy:\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[rhoN, chiN, resN, r];\ndiscreteFixedPointEqns = Thread[\n   \
opfMap[{rhoN, chiN, resN}, r] == {rhoN, chiN, resN}\n   ];\n\n\
discreteFixedPoints = Reduce[\n   discreteFixedPointEqns,\n   {rhoN, chiN, \
resN},\n   Reals,\n   MaxExtraConditions -> All\n   ];\n\ndiscreteFixedPoints\
\n```\n\nThe linearized recursion Jacobian is:\n\n```wl\njacobian = \
D[opfMap[{rhoN, chiN, resN}, r], {{rhoN, chiN, resN}}] // FullSimplify;\n\
jacobian // MatrixForm\n```\n\nLocal discrete stability requires eigenvalues \
of the Jacobian to lie on or within the unit circle depending on the \
strictness desired.\n\n```wl\ndiscreteEigenvalues = Eigenvalues[jacobian] // \
FullSimplify;\ndiscreteEigenvalues\n```\n\nFor a specific background, \
substitute a fixed point and test the spectrum as a function of ratio ``r``.\n\
\n---\n\n## 12. Mapping missing variables relative to the Standard Model\n\n\
The user's directive asks to identify variables not contained in standard \
textbook parameter sets. In this symbolic OPF formalism, the primitive \
variables are:\n\n```wl\nopfVariables = {rho, chi, res, sigma, alpha, beta, \
gamma, eta, zeta, xi, delta, lambda};\nopfVariables\n```\n\nThese are \
conceptual rather than Standard Model constants. The key point is that OPF \
introduces:\n\n- intrinsic information density,\n- intrinsic field \
complexity,\n- observational resolution as a dynamical quantity,\n- explicit \
scale recursion,\n- self-measurement kernels.\n\nThese are not part of \
conventional Standard Model parameterization.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Relevant \
adjacent areas this framework could influence\n\nThe following domains are \
mathematically adjacent to the OPF program and can be explored by translating \
OPF structures into their native formal language.\n\n### 13.1 Renormalization \
and multiscale field theory\n\nThe scale coordinate ``sigma`` and recursive \
structure connect naturally to renormalization group ideas, but with the \
important difference that here measurement itself is dynamical.\n\nPotential \
influence:\n\n- reinterpretation of RG flow as self-measurement flow,\n- \
fixed points as measurement-stable ontological sectors,\n- scale anomalies as \
breakdown of recursive self-consistency.\n\n### 13.2 Quantum foundations\n\n\
The self-measurement fixed-point condition could be compared against:\n\n- \
decoherence,\n- consistent histories,\n- relational quantum mechanics,\n- \
quantum Darwinism.\n\nPotential influence:\n\n- measurement kernel as \
alternative ontology for classicality emergence,\n- observables as \
fixed-point projections of recursive field structure.\n\n### 13.3 Information \
geometry and variational inference\n\nThe term ``rho Log[chi]`` suggests an \
informational variational principle.\n\nPotential influence:\n\n- \
interpreting OPF stationary points as extremizers of information-complexity \
tradeoff,\n- defining Fisher-like metrics on OPF state space,\n- treating \
scale recursion as hierarchical inference.\n\n### 13.4 Dynamical systems and \
self-reference\n\nThe recursion map and self-measurement operator relate to:\n\
\n- nonlinear fixed-point theory,\n- self-referential systems,\n- \
category-theoretic endomorphisms,\n- recursive graph transforms.\n\nPotential \
influence:\n\n- rigorous classification of self-sustaining measurement \
structures,\n- attractor theory for ontological persistence.\n\n### 13.5 \
Quasicrystals, phyllotaxis, and optimal irrational packing\n\nGolden-ratio \
and golden-angle structures appear in low-overlap packing and recursive \
nonresonance.\n\nPotential influence:\n\n- identifying measurement lattices \
or quasi-lattices in scale-space,\n- understanding why certain irrational \
ratios optimize recursive stability.\n\n### 13.6 Consciousness modeling, \
cautiously framed\n\nIf consciousness is treated as recursive self-modeling \
rather than presumed solved, OPF could influence formal models of:\n\n- \
self-reference,\n- integrated recursive inference,\n- multiscale internal \
observability.\n\nPotential influence:\n\n- necessary conditions for \
self-representing systems,\n- stability criteria for recursive awareness \
maps.\n\nThis remains speculative and requires independent criteria beyond \
the present notebook.\n\n### 13.7 PDE scaling and nondimensionalization\n\n\
Any actual numerical realization of OPF must respect scale alignment. This \
connects directly to \
[NondimensionalizationTransform](paclet:ref/NondimensionalizationTransform) \
and to PDE scaling best practices.\n\nPotential influence:\n\n- choosing \
natural OPF units,\n- avoiding numerically meaningless regimes,\n- testing \
whether Planck-like scales are true singular structures or merely poor \
coordinate choices.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Suggested next calculations\n\n1. \
**Exact fixed points**: specialize parameters and solve ``fixedPoints`` \
explicitly.\n2. **Ratio scan**: insert candidate ``r`` values into \
``jacobian`` and rank by spectral stability.\n3. **Kernel spectra**: choose \
an explicit ``kernel`` and solve the self-measurement eigenproblem.\n4. \
**Nondimensionalization**: construct a natural unit system for OPF variables.\
\n5. **Higher dimensions**: replace ``x`` by ``{x1, x2, x3}`` and extend the \
action.\n6. **Emergent time**: add a slow-time variable only at Layer 2 and \
compare with the timeless Layer 1 sector.\n7. **Homomorphism pipeline**: \
implement explicit maps ``L0 -> L1 -> L2`` as callable functions.\n\n---\n\n\
## 15. Minimal callable pipeline\n\n```wl\nClearAll[deriveFieldEquations, \
homogeneousSector, linearizedSector, ratioData];\n\nderiveFieldEquations[] := \
fieldEquations;\n\nhomogeneousSector[] := <|\n   \"Equations\" -> \
homogeneousEquations,\n   \"FixedPoints\" -> fixedPoints\n   |>;\n\n\
linearizedSector[] := <|\n   \"LinearizedEquations\" -> linearizedFirstOrder,\
\n   \"ModalMatrix\" -> modalMatrix,\n   \"DispersionRelation\" -> \
dispersionRelation,\n   \"UndampedModeConditions\" -> undampedModeConditions\n\
   |>;\n\nratioData[] := <|\n   \"CandidateRatios\" -> candidateRatios,\n   \
\"RatioScores\" -> ratioScores,\n   \"RatioSteps\" -> ratioSteps,\n   \
\"Jacobian\" -> jacobian,\n   \"DiscreteEigenvalues\" -> discreteEigenvalues\n\
   |>;\n```\n\nThese functions expose the OPF derivation as a reusable \
symbolic pipeline.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Closing note\n\nThis notebook gives a \
rigorous starting point for investigating the user's core intuition:\n\n- a \
timeless substrate,\n- recursive self-measurement across scales,\n- \
fixed-point reality as stable self-consistency,\n- distinguished irrational \
ratios as candidate nonresonant recursive structures.\n\nThe appropriate next \
phase is **not** to claim success, but to test:\n\n- whether nontrivial fixed \
points exist,\n- whether they are stable,\n- whether special ratios actually \
optimize anything,\n- whether the measurement operator admits physically \
interpretable eigenstructures.",
